[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memory Profiling and Tuning in Go (Golang)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go is equipped with a powerful runtime and garbage collector, but high-performance applications still require diligent memory management. Memory profiling helps identify memory leaks, excessive allocations, and garbage collection pressure."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Getting Started with pprof"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The standard library provides the runtime/pprof and net/http/pprof packages to collect and analyze memory profiles. For web servers, importing net/http/pprof automatically registers handlers under /debug/pprof/."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"package main

import (
	"log"
	"net/http"
	_ "net/http/pprof"
)

func main() {
	// Starts a background server for profiling
	go func() {
		log.Println(http.ListenAndServe("localhost:6060", nil))
	}()

	// Application logic goes here
	select {}
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Capturing and Analyzing Heap Profiles"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can capture a live heap profile using the Go toolchain via your terminal. Run the following command to open the interactive CLI:"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"go tool pprof http://localhost:6060/debug/pprof/heap"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the "top" command inside the pprof shell to see functions consuming the most memory."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the "web" command to generate a visual SVG graph of memory allocations (requires Graphviz)."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Profile Metrics"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"When analyzing heap profiles, you will encounter four primary metrics:"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"inuse_space: Amount of memory currently allocated and not yet freed."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"inuse_objects: Number of objects currently allocated."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"alloc_space: Total memory allocated throughout the lifetime of the program."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"alloc_objects: Total number of objects allocated throughout the lifetime of the program."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Techniques for Memory Tuning"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once you identify allocation hot spots, apply these tuning strategies to reduce pressure on the garbage collector:"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use sync.Pool to reuse frequently created objects and reduce heap allocation overhead."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Preallocate slices with known capacities using make([]T, 0, capacity) to avoid repeated reallocation and copying."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Minimize pointer usage in large structs to help the garbage collector scan memory faster."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Tuning Garbage Collection"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can control the garbage collector's aggressiveness by setting the GOGC environment variable. The default value is 100, meaning a new GC cycle triggers when heap growth reaches 100% of the live heap size from the previous cycle. Lowering GOGC increases CPU usage for more frequent collections, while raising it uses more memory for fewer collections."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"export GOGC=50"}]
